menu "Prusa Remote Display UI"

    config PRD_UI_RENDER_REPORT
        bool "Log renderer flush, memory and stack measurements periodically"
        default n
        help
            Emits the flush-path, LVGL-pool, heap, task-stack, presentation-intake
            and scene-resolution figures every 15 seconds under the prd.ui tag.

            This is measurement, not product behaviour, and it is what every
            draw-buffer and pool-size decision in the project rests on. It is
            deliberately independent of which scene is compiled: the figures are as
            useful over the product composition as they were over the Milestone 1
            bring-up scene, and tying them to a bench scene would mean the product
            scene could never be profiled.

            Enable in the hardware-validation profile. Leave off for debug and
            release, where a log line every 15 seconds is noise.

    config PRD_UI_SCENE_ANNOTATION
        bool "Annotate presentations that have no composition yet"
        default n
        help
            Draws a small line of metadata-sized text naming the presentation layer,
            subject state and confidence whenever the scene controller resolves a
            presentation it has no composition for.

            WHY THIS EXISTS. Production UI Specification §9 defines sixteen screens and
            Milestone 2 Phase C implements one of them, so most presentations resolve to
            nothing and the panel shows `color.background` alone. On a bench that is
            indistinguishable from a dead render task, a lost flush completion, a panic
            halt with the backlight still on, or a backlight fault — which makes a black
            panel useless as evidence and, worse, able to hide a real fault.

            With this on, black means broken and text means "no composition yet, and here
            is which one was asked for".

            This is a bench annotation, not a product placeholder. It carries no printer
            data, invents nothing, and is compiled out of debug and release — Production
            UI §9 forbids simulated and placeholder data, and a "coming soon" panel in a
            product image would be exactly that.

    config PRD_UI_DIAGNOSTIC_SCENE
        bool "Replace the product scene with the LVGL foundation diagnostic scene"
        default n
        # The scene draws Montserrat deliberately: it is LVGL's built-in, so what it
        # exercises is the renderer and not the project's own generated faces. The base
        # defaults switch Montserrat off because no product image should carry a
        # typeface nothing draws, so the scene selects back exactly what it uses.
        select LV_FONT_MONTSERRAT_14
        select LV_FONT_MONTSERRAT_48
        help
            Compiles the Milestone 1 Phase F bring-up scene in place of the product
            composition: a deliberately non-product screen that exercises every
            rendering primitive the accepted Production UI Specification needs — an
            anti-aliased round-capped arc, large and small text, a rapidly updating
            numeric value, an object clipped by the canvas edge, and the accepted
            background and accent colours as colour probes.

            It remains available because it is validated hardware-bring-up
            diagnostics: when a panel, a draw buffer or a flush path is suspect, a
            scene with known-correct expected output is worth more than a product
            screen whose content depends on the whole application stack.

            MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE WITH THE PRODUCT SCENE, AT COMPILE TIME. Both build
            on the display's active screen and strip its style, so two builders would
            interleave and neither could be held responsible for the result. Selecting
            this option compiles the product scene controller out entirely rather
            than leaving a run-time precedence rule that no test can check.

            While it is selected the application store still runs and presentations
            are still submitted; they are received and counted, and nothing is drawn
            from them.

            This is diagnostics, not product behaviour. It must stay off in debug and
            release so no bench artwork can reach a product image, and off in the
            normal hardware-validation flow so that what is validated is the product
            composition.

endmenu
